        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Cycle Ergometer (CE) has a standard flywheel and braking-band system, with workload controlled by a workload control panel, computer-driven exercise protocol, or manual control.  It was developed as an exercise device for preventing cardiovascular deconditioning and reduction in leg strength resulting from exposure to a microgravity environment during spaceflight. Cycle exercise is anticipated to result in lower vibration and acoustic levels than have been experienced using the Shuttle treadmill. Testing on board the KC-135 during parabolic flights showed that a recumbent cycling position is extremely stable while using only a seat back to transfer the leg loads directly to the mounting frame.
<P>
The CE is designed to be mounted to the treadmill ascent/entry floor studs on the Orbiter middeck for launch and landing. Its standard flywheel and braking-band system generates the required resistive force, and resistance is controlled by a stepper motor that regulates braking-band tension. The Accessories Case contains all the cycle accessories, cycling shoes, and clothes needed to perform cycle exercise on the Shuttle.
<P>
Workload can be automatically controlled using a workload control panel or a computer-driven exercise protocol. The cycle workload can also be controlled (0 to 350 watts in 25-watt increments) using a manual control similar to those found on exercise cycles made for use at home or in gymnasiums. The workload control panel also displays deviation from the desired pedal cadence [50 to 120 revolutions per minute (rpm) in 5-rpm increments].
<P>
The CE has a serial data port that provides the capability to receive commands and be controlled from an external computer, allowing predefined protocols to be executed automatically. Data such as achieved workload and rpm can be continuously recorded on the computer during execution of the protocols. The CE is designed to operate on 28 volts of direct current at 15 watts.
<P>
The mounting frame was a major design challenge. For launch, it has to mate to the middeck floor using the same attachment points (mounting studs of the type used in military cargo aircraft) used by the Shuttle Treadmill. Because the launch location is not suitable for on-orbit exercise, the frame must be reconfigured on orbit to attach to floor studs in a standard chair pattern on either the flight deck or middeck. Using a mounting frame that can be reconfigured minimizes launch weight and volume because the launch frame satisfies all of the structural requirements for both launching the cycle system and recumbent cycling in microgravity. The frame that has been designed is a lightweight, two-piece frame that can be easily reconfigured by crew members in orbit.
<P>
For the launch configuration, the CE load module lays flat on its side (to lower the center of gravity) on the two halves of the mounting frame. The load module and seat back stem become structural members for launch. For operation on orbit, the positions of the two mounting frame halves are reversed, and the CE load module is placed upright on top of them. The pedals, control panel, and power cable are connected to the CE. The seat back is positioned on the seat stem and is set at a comfortable angle and distance from the pedals.<END>
        </div>

